# or-is-leftmost-first: `a or b` prefers `a`, so a search takes the first
# alternative that matches at the leftmost start, not the longest.
# `<"a" or "ab">` finds "a" twice in "ab abc", and `<"ab" or "a">` finds
# "ab" twice. A whole-text match still takes the most preferred way that
# matches all of the text, so "ab" matches `<"a" or "ab">` (spec chapter 4,
# Matching; ADR 0007). The matches and the boolean are pinned. Its Fuel,
# allocation and state figures are Cost Model 0's, as the TS Core blessed them.

> load search identity=e76eb17050868fa079506ed9230ee62d3d9b718a982a1821ec0b0854dbed55ae

# Steps: 22 for the first Match Search, 20 for the second, and 5 for the
# whole-text match, which stops at its first match.
> deliver d1 to=search message=go
> pump clock=2026-09-30T09:00:00Z
seg search/r1 start delivery=d1 handler=go clause=1 fuel=82 alloc=994 state=1002 end=return
run search/r1 outcome=completed delivery=d1 handler=go fuel=82 alloc=994
pumped state=idle fuel=82

> vars
vars search short=[{text: "a", range: 1..1, captures: {}, ranges: {}}, {text: "a", range: 4..4, captures: {}, ranges: {}}] long=[{text: "ab", range: 1..2, captures: {}, ranges: {}}, {text: "ab", range: 4..5, captures: {}, ranges: {}}] whole=true
